-- Verified revenue - defending a contested number over time, with the
-- rules about who may rely on it enforced by the runtime instead of by
-- combing through old emails.
--
-- A quick tour of the domain. Picture a battery-storage system - the
-- kind of asset that charges when power is cheap and sells when it is
-- dear. Each month it earns revenue, and several parties care about that
-- one figure for different reasons: the owner reports it, the bank that
-- financed the battery tests it against loan covenants, investor
-- relations puts it in a shareholder deck, sometimes a regulator looks
-- at it. The number is the same; what differs is what each party is
-- allowed to *do* with it.
--
-- Two things make this hard, and this example is built around both.
--
-- First, the figure can be corrected. A verifier signs off on, say, Q1
-- revenue; weeks later, after the meter data is reconciled, they revise
-- it. The honest way to handle that is not to overwrite the old number -
-- "what did we report at the time?" must always have a truthful answer.
-- Instead the old figure stays on the record, a fresh corrected figure
-- is admitted alongside it, a pointer to "the figure currently in force"
-- moves to the new one, and a lineage link records that the new one
-- supersedes the old.
--
-- Second, relying on the figure takes permission. A party does not just
-- use the number; an authority grants it *standing* to be relied on for
-- a specific purpose - the bank's credit committee grants standing for
-- covenant tests, investor relations grants standing for shareholder
-- reporting. Two separate permissions on the same figure, from two
-- different authorities, each revocable without touching the other.
--
-- Where the two meet is the crux. When the verifier corrects the figure,
-- every standing granted on the old figure is withdrawn - the
-- authorities must re-grant on the corrected figure if they still accept
-- it. But every decision already made under the old standing survives
-- untouched: a covenant test the bank ran in good faith in June is a
-- true record of what the bank decided that day, even after the figure
-- is corrected in July. Correction removes future reliance on the old
-- figure; it never rewrites the past.
--
-- Here is how Morpholog thinks about this. It does not store "facts" -
-- it stores *claims* it has agreed to admit because they obey the rules.
-- Two kinds of rule do the work below:
--
--   * an `invariant` is a rule that must hold for all admitted state,
--     forever. The runtime refuses to commit any change that would
--     break one - for example, a live permission can never exist without
--     a grant behind it.
--   * a `require` is a gate on a single action, checked at the moment
--     you act and never again. The distinction is the whole lesson of
--     this example: whether a decision was allowed is settled when the
--     decision is made and stays settled. If "every decision needs live
--     standing" were an invariant instead of a gate, then revoking
--     standing later would force the runtime either to refuse the
--     revocation or to erase the decisions made under it - both wrong.
--     An invariant governs what is true; a gate governs what you may do.

program verified_revenue

-- The vocabulary. Each `predicate` names a kind of claim this world can
-- hold, and now SAYS which kind it is. Some are append-only records
-- (the figures, the grants, the revocations, the decisions) - history
-- is never rewritten, and `append only` makes the runtime refuse any
-- transformation that would try. Others are retractable pointers that
-- move as the world changes; `current pointer by` declares the
-- singleton ("one figure in force per asset and period") and
-- `superseded via` names the lineage chain, which can then never fork.
predicate IndependentlyVerifiedRevenue(asset: Subject, period: Subject, amount: Decimal, verification_id: Subject)
    append only
predicate CurrentVerification(asset: Subject, period: Subject, verification_id: Subject)
    current pointer by (asset, period)
    superseded via Supersedes
predicate Supersedes(new_verification_id: Subject, prior_verification_id: Subject)
predicate StandingGrantedBy(verification_id: Subject, purpose: Subject, authority: Subject, grant_id: Subject)
    append only
predicate AdmissibleFor(verification_id: Subject, purpose: Subject)
predicate StandingRevoked(verification_id: Subject, purpose: Subject, revocation_id: Subject)
    append only
predicate DebtServiceRevenue(asset: Subject, period: Subject, amount: Decimal, decision_id: Subject, verification_id: Subject)
    append only
predicate InvestorReportedRevenue(asset: Subject, period: Subject, amount: Decimal, report_id: Subject, verification_id: Subject)
    append only

-- Intents are messages the system sends to the outside world after a
-- change commits - a figure was verified, a figure was corrected, a
-- permission was granted or revoked, a decision was recorded. They are
-- how this corner of the business announces what just happened to
-- everyone downstream who is waiting on it.
intent IndependentVerificationAdmitted(verification_id: Subject)
intent VerificationCorrected(new_verification_id: Subject, prior_verification_id: Subject)
intent StandingGranted(grant_id: Subject)
intent StandingRevocationAdmitted(revocation_id: Subject)
intent DebtServiceRevenueAdmitted(decision_id: Subject)
intent InvestorReportedRevenueAdmitted(report_id: Subject)


-- The rules that must always hold. If any change below would leave the
-- world violating one of these, the whole change is rejected. Read
-- `implies` as "whenever the left is true, the right must be too".

-- A live permission can never float free: every active "admissible for
-- this purpose" claim must have a grant standing behind it. You cannot
-- end up able to rely on a figure with no record of who let you.
invariant admissibility_has_provenance:
    AdmissibleFor(v, p) implies StandingGrantedBy(v, p, _, _)


-- A permission and its own revocation cannot both be live at once. Once
-- standing for a purpose is revoked, the matching "admissible for" claim
-- must be gone.
invariant admissibility_excludes_revocation:
    AdmissibleFor(v, p) implies not (exists r: StandingRevoked(v, p, r))



-- The only ways state may change. A transformation proposes a set of
-- changes; the runtime applies them only if every invariant still holds
-- once they are in place. `admit` adds a claim; `retract` removes one.

-- The first time a figure is verified for an asset and period. It
-- records the figure and sets the "current" pointer to it. The gate
-- refuses if a current figure already exists for this asset and period -
-- a brand-new figure goes through the correction path instead, never by
-- silently replacing what is already in force.
transformation admit_independent_verification(
    asset, period, amount, verification_id
):
    require not CurrentVerification(asset, period, _)
    admit IndependentlyVerifiedRevenue(asset, period, amount, verification_id)
    admit CurrentVerification(asset, period, verification_id)
    emit IndependentVerificationAdmitted(verification_id)


-- Correct a figure the honest way. The original figure stays on the
-- record; the corrected figure is admitted alongside it; a lineage link
-- records that the new one supersedes the old; the "current" pointer
-- moves to the new figure. The gate refuses to correct a figure that has
-- already been superseded, so the chain never forks. Crucially, every
-- live permission on the old figure is withdrawn here - the `_` means
-- "every purpose", so all standing on the prior figure goes at once.
-- Authorities must re-grant on the corrected figure if they accept it.
-- Decisions already made under the old standing are left untouched.
transformation correct_independent_verification(
    asset, period, new_amount, new_verification_id, prior_verification_id
):
    require IndependentlyVerifiedRevenue(asset, period, _, prior_verification_id)
    require not Supersedes(_, prior_verification_id)
    admit IndependentlyVerifiedRevenue(asset, period, new_amount, new_verification_id)
    admit Supersedes(new_verification_id, prior_verification_id)
    retract CurrentVerification(asset, period, prior_verification_id)
    retract AdmissibleFor(prior_verification_id, _)
    admit CurrentVerification(asset, period, new_verification_id)
    emit VerificationCorrected(new_verification_id, prior_verification_id)


-- An authority grants standing for a figure to be relied on for a
-- purpose. The gates make sure it is sound: the figure must exist, it
-- must be the one currently in force (you cannot grant new standing on a
-- superseded figure - future reliance attaches to the live figure), the
-- purpose must not already be revoked, and it must not already be live.
-- Recording both the grant (who permitted it) and the live permission
-- keeps the provenance invariant satisfied.
transformation grant_standing(
    verification_id, purpose, authority, grant_id
):
    require IndependentlyVerifiedRevenue(_, _, _, verification_id)
    require CurrentVerification(_, _, verification_id)
    require not (exists r: StandingRevoked(verification_id, purpose, r))
    require not AdmissibleFor(verification_id, purpose)
    admit StandingGrantedBy(verification_id, purpose, authority, grant_id)
    admit AdmissibleFor(verification_id, purpose)
    emit StandingGranted(grant_id)


-- Withdraw a standing. The gate requires that the permission is
-- currently live; the live permission is removed and a revocation is
-- recorded. The revocation is permanent - there is no transformation to
-- undo it - which is why the invariants above can treat a revoked
-- purpose as closed for good.
transformation revoke_standing(verification_id, purpose, revocation_id):
    require AdmissibleFor(verification_id, purpose)
    retract AdmissibleFor(verification_id, purpose)
    admit StandingRevoked(verification_id, purpose, revocation_id)
    emit StandingRevocationAdmitted(revocation_id)


-- Record a bank debt-service decision that relies on a figure. The gates
-- run at the moment of the decision: the figure must match, and there
-- must be live standing for this specific purpose. The purpose is
-- written right into the gate. Once admitted, the decision is a permanent
-- record - later revoking the standing does not, and must not, reach back
-- and invalidate the decision that was legitimate when it was made.
transformation admit_debt_service_revenue(
    asset, period, amount, decision_id, verification_id
):
    require IndependentlyVerifiedRevenue(asset, period, amount, verification_id)
    require AdmissibleFor(verification_id, #bank_debt_service)
    admit DebtServiceRevenue(asset, period, amount, decision_id, verification_id)
    emit DebtServiceRevenueAdmitted(decision_id)


-- The same shape for an investor-reporting decision: a matching figure
-- and live standing for the investor-reporting purpose, gated at the
-- moment the figure goes into the report. The two decision paths are
-- deliberately parallel - same figure, different purposes, independent
-- permissions.
transformation admit_investor_reported_revenue(
    asset, period, amount, report_id, verification_id
):
    require IndependentlyVerifiedRevenue(asset, period, amount, verification_id)
    require AdmissibleFor(verification_id, #investor_reporting)
    admit InvestorReportedRevenue(asset, period, amount, report_id, verification_id)
    emit InvestorReportedRevenueAdmitted(report_id)
